yes — and there is one especially good find in the manual:
LSM6 is exactly “touch panel is currently pressed.” It is 1 while the touch panel is being pressed and 0 otherwise.     IDEC_WINDOI_NV4_InternalDocs-1

That immediately moves LSM6 very high on the read-test list, because if we can read it remotely you could detect a human touch in real time.

The other things you remembered are there too:
- LSD51 is the brightness level, and the manual explicitly says brightness can be adjusted by changing LSD51.     IDEC_WINDOI_NV4_InternalDocs-1
- LSD227 reports which monitor/maintenance/pass-through TCP functions are in use.     IDEC_WINDOI_NV4_InternalDocs-1
- Touch sound is definitely configurable. The project can enable/disable touch sound, select beep vs sound file, and optionally control whether touch sound plays using a device address whose value is 0 = no sound, 1 = play sound.     IDEC_WINDOI_NV4_InternalDocs-1

The touch-sound setting is a little different from LSD51: I have not yet found a fixed LSDxxx that simply means “touch sound enabled.” The manual describes it primarily as a project setting, with an optional user-selected device address controlling it. So whether we can remotely determine it may depend on how the project was configured.     IDEC_WINDOI_NV4_InternalDocs-1
The really interesting unresolved question is coordinates.
